{#-
  Generated sitemap, built from src/_data/publicRoutes.js — the same inventory the
  deployment audit, the crawler and the validator use. It is NOT a scan of dist/,
  so a diagnostic or fixture page cannot slip in by existing.

  `lastmod` is deliberately OMITTED. The live file carries one hand-set date on all
  22 URLs, which is not a per-URL content date, and there is no authoritative
  deterministic date to replace it with: most of these pages have no content date
  at all. Deriving one from the build clock, file mtime or the current date would
  make the sitemap non-deterministic AND tell search engines something untrue on
  every deploy. Omitting the field says nothing rather than something false.
  Recorded as an approved difference in docs/CUTOVER_READINESS.md §3.

  `changefreq` and `priority` ARE kept: the live values are a consistent, evidently
  intentional tier model, and a cutover phase is the wrong moment to change a
  published signal.

  No `xhtml:link` alternates. Every generated page already carries a reciprocal
  hreflang trio with an English x-default in its <head>, which is the canonical
  place for it; duplicating that in the sitemap adds a second thing to keep
  consistent for no gain. The live sitemap has none either.
-#}
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<urlset xmlns="http://www.sitemaps.org/schemas/sitemap/0.9">
{%- for locale in locales %}

  <!-- {{ "English (default)" if locale.code == "en" else "Polish" }} -->
{%- for route in publicRoutes.routes %}
{%- if route.locale == locale.code %}
  <url>
    <loc>{{ publicRoutes.domain }}{{ route.loc }}</loc>
    <changefreq>{{ route.changefreq }}</changefreq>
    <priority>{{ route.priority }}</priority>
  </url>
{%- endif %}
{%- endfor %}
{%- endfor %}

</urlset>
